 \section*{Abstract}
-\noindent
-\lipsum[1] % Generates a sample dummy paragraph
+Our work proposes a framework for improving template discovery in RNA 3D structure prediction. Building on RNAPro, whose accuracy depends strongly on the quality of its input templates, we improve how templates are chosen at inference. Several independent search methods, covering sequence, family, embedding, secondary-structure, and motif similarity, generate candidate templates. A learned selector, trained on TM-align labels, then ranks them, with a zero-valued template as fallback when none is useful. We also compare RNA language models within the same pipeline under controlled conditions. Results will be compared against the paper's baselines and its best-template upper bound. 
+\pagebreak 
+
+\section*{Executive Summary}
+ 
+\subsection*{Background and Problem}
+ 
+Predicting the 3D structure of RNA is difficult, and accuracy depends heavily on the quality of the structural templates supplied to a prediction model. Our baseline, RNAPro (Lee et al., 2025), extends the Protenix AlphaFold 3 reproduction with multiple sequence alignments, an RNA language model, and 3D templates. Its authors found that templates are the model's most important input, that accuracy tracks the best available template, and that different template-search strategies succeed on different targets. This project addresses that gap: how to find and choose better templates for each RNA.
+ 
+\subsection*{Proposed Approach}
+ 
+We keep RNAPro's architecture and training recipe fixed, so any improvement can be attributed to better template choice rather than retraining. Our contribution is a template-selection layer applied at inference, made up of three parts:
+ 
+\begin{enumerate}
+
+
+  \item \textbf{Candidate generation.} Five independent search methods propose templates for a target RNA: sequence similarity (including Rfam/Infernal and MMseqs2), function or family lookup, language-model embedding similarity, secondary-structure-aware similarity, and motif or local template search. The first-place Kaggle method used in the paper is included as a baseline source.
+  \item \textbf{Learned selector.} Candidates are pooled and scored by a ranking or deep-learning model trained to predict each template's TM-align against the experimental structure, computed with US-align on the C1$'$ atom. Near-duplicates are removed, and the top five templates are passed to the trained model, which produces five predictions. If no candidate scores above a threshold tuned on validation data, a zero-valued template is used, as RNAPro does.
+  \item \textbf{Language-model comparison.} Under controlled conditions (same data, template pool, seeds, and schedule), we test alternative RNA language models, such as AIDO.RNA, in the slot occupied by the default RNet2, and select the best on validation data.
+\end{enumerate}
+ 
+\subsection*{Data and Evaluation}
+ 
+We use PDB structures with C1$'$ coordinates, rMSA-generated MSAs, and the Eigen dataset for the second training stage. The primary benchmark uses a temporal cutoff, as in the paper Template searches for all targets run only against structures that are legal under the split, and test targets are scored once.
+ 
+Performance is measured by TM-align (best of five predictions per target, averaged across targets), with a score above 0.45 indicating a correct global fold. We also report TM-score, C1$'$ RMSD, and per-target Z-scores. Results are compared against reference points from the paper:
+ 
+\begin{itemize}
+  \item MMseqs2-only baseline: 0.321
+  \item Best prior PDB template (upper bound): 0.679
+  \item RNAPro's own reported mean: 0.640 in the text and 0.648 in Table 2, so we will compare against our own reproduced baseline instead
+\end{itemize}
+ 
+Template-poor targets are reported separately, and confidence intervals or significance tests are used where the test set size allows.
+ 
+\subsection*{Limitations}
+ 
+The RNAPro results are retrospective, and a prospective blind test is still needed; our evaluation inherits this limitation. Because accuracy is bounded by the best available template, a selector can improve results only up to that ceiling, and we will report how close it comes. The paper's small test set (ten RNA-Puzzles targets) limits statistical power. Full retraining requires substantial compute (the paper used eight GB300 GPUs), so if it proves infeasible we will report the best available alternative and state this clearly.
+ 
+\subsection*{Expected Contribution}
+ 
+This work tests whether pooling diverse template-search methods and choosing among them with a learned selector improves RNA 3D structure prediction over single-method search, and how much of the gap to the best-template upper bound it can close.
