+\chapter{Introduction}
+
+RNA molecules do far more than carry genetic messages. They regulate genes, catalyze reactions, and form the machinery of the cell, and what they do depends on how they fold in three dimensions. Experimentally determining an RNA structure is slow and expensive, so computational prediction is an active research area. Deep learning has transformed protein structure prediction, but RNA remains harder. Few experimentally solved RNA structures exist, and the molecules are flexible and often adopt several conformations.\\
+
+One recent model for this problem is RNAPro (Lee et al., 2025). It extends the Protenix reproduction of AlphaFold 3 with multiple sequence alignments, an RNA language model, and 3D structural templates. Its authors found that templates are its most important input. They also found that accuracy follows the quality of the best available template, and that different template-search methods succeed on different RNAs. No single search method works reliably for every target, and the model has no way to decide which templates to trust.\\
+
+This project addresses that gap. We keep RNAPro's architecture and training recipe fixed and change only how templates are found and chosen at inference. Several independent search methods propose candidates, and a learned selector ranks them by their expected structural accuracy. We also compare several RNA language models inside the same pipeline. Keeping the model fixed lets us attribute any improvement to better template choice rather than retraining.
+
+\section{Purpose of this Document}
+This document presents the design, methodology, and planned evaluation of our Final Year Project (FYP), which aims to improve template discovery for RNA 3D structure prediction. The project is guided by the following research questions:
+\begin{enumerate}
+    \item Does pooling template candidates from several independent search methods, and choosing among them with a learned selector, improve prediction accuracy over a single search method?
+    \item How much of the gap between the baseline and the best-template upper bound can such a selector close?
+    \item How do different RNA language models compare when placed in the same pipeline under controlled conditions?
+    \item How does performance change on template-poor RNAs, which existing benchmarks do not cover?
+\end{enumerate}
+The report describes the background and related work, the proposed framework, the data and splits, the evaluation protocol, and the system through which researchers submit RNA sequences and inspect predicted structures. It also states the limitations of the work. The main limits are that the RNAPro results we build on are retrospective, that accuracy is capped by the best available template, and that full retraining needs substantial compute.
+
+\section{Intended Audience}
+This report is written primarily for the researchers in computational biology who might use the resulting tool. It is also meant for future students or researchers who may extend it. Readers are assumed to know machine learning and software engineering. Because RNA structure prediction is a specialized area, the report explains the biological and structural concepts it relies on, and Section~\ref{sec:definitions} defines the key terms. The FYP evaluation panel of faculty members, who will assess the technical soundness, novelty, and completeness of the work, is a secondary audience.
+
+\section{Definitions, Acronyms, and Abbreviations}
+\label{sec:definitions}
+This section defines the key terms, acronyms and abbreviations used throughout this document.\\
+\textbf{SDG}: Sustainable Development Goal\\
