+\textbf{REST}: Representational State Transfer\\
+\textbf{ORM}: Object-Relational Mapping\\
+\textbf{CRUD}: Create, Read, Update, Delete\\
+\textbf{API}: Application Programming Interface\\
+\textbf{2D}: Two-dimensional\\
+\textbf{3D}: Three-dimensional\\
+\textbf{3D Viewer}: The in-browser tool (Mol* or NGL) used to display and interact with a predicted 3D structure.\\
+\textbf{AIDO.RNA}: One of the RNA language models compared in this work.\\
+\textbf{Baseline Score}: The score of a model before the re-ranker is applied, shown next to the re-ranker score so the user can see the effect of re-ranking.\\
+\textbf{C1$'$ atom}: The carbon atom of the ribose sugar used to represent each nucleotide when comparing RNA structures.\\
+\textbf{Candidate (Top-k)}: One of the k best predicted models for a sequence, ranked by the re-ranker.\\
+\textbf{Confidence Indicator}: A simple low, medium or high flag with a short reason, which tells the user how far a predicted model can be trusted.\\
+\textbf{Dot-Bracket Notation}: A text representation of a secondary structure in which matching brackets mark paired nucleotides and dots mark unpaired nucleotides, e.g., \texttt{((((...))))}.\\
+\textbf{Job}: A single prediction request submitted by the user, which runs in the background and produces one or more predicted models.\\
+\textbf{Loop}: A group of unpaired nucleotides enclosed by, or connecting, stems.\\
+\textbf{MMseqs2}: A fast sequence search tool used as the baseline template search.\\
+\textbf{Model (3D Structure)}: A predicted three-dimensional arrangement of the atoms of an RNA molecule, generated by the prediction pipeline.\\
+\textbf{MSA}: Multiple Sequence Alignment, an alignment of related sequences that shows which positions are conserved.\\
+\textbf{PDB}: Protein Data Bank, a standard text file format for storing 3D molecular coordinates, which can be opened in external molecular tools.\\
+\textbf{Pseudoknot}: A structural element in which base pairs cross each other, so that it cannot be drawn as a simple nested structure.\\
+\textbf{Re-ranker}: The machine learning layer that scores the candidate models and reorders them so that the most likely models appear first.\\
+\textbf{Researcher}: The end user of the system, who submits RNA sequences and inspects the predicted structures.\\
+\textbf{Rfam / Infernal}: A database of RNA families and the tool used to search it by sequence and secondary structure.\\
+\textbf{RMSD}: Root Mean Square Deviation, the average distance between corresponding atoms in two structures.\\
+\textbf{RNA}: Ribonucleic Acid\\
+\textbf{RNAPro}: The baseline structure prediction model on which this work builds.\\
+\textbf{RNet2}: One of the RNA language models used or compared in this work.\\
+\textbf{Secondary Structure}: The 2D pattern of base pairs formed when an RNA sequence folds, made up of stems, loops and pseudoknots.\\
+\textbf{Selector}: The learned model that scores candidate templates and picks the most trustworthy ones for each RNA.\\
+\textbf{Stem}: A stretch of consecutive base-paired nucleotides, shown as a double-stranded region in the 2D graph.\\
+\textbf{SVG}: Scalable Vector Graphics, the image format used to draw the interactive 2D secondary structure graph.\\
+\textbf{Template}: A known RNA structure that is used as a starting point for building the prediction of a new sequence.\\
+\textbf{Template-poor target}: An RNA with no close structural match in the available database.\\
+\textbf{Temporal split}: A data split where only structures released before a target's cutoff date may be used as templates or training data.\\
+\textbf{TM-align / TM-score}: A measure of structural similarity between two 3D structures, from 0 to 1. A score above 0.45 is treated as a correct global fold.\\
+\textbf{US-align}: The tool used to compute TM-align for RNA structures.\\
+\textbf{WebGL}: Web Graphics Library, the browser technology used to render 3D graphics.\\
