+\chapter{Project Vision}
+
+\section{Problem Domain Overview}
+An RNA molecule's function depends on its three-dimensional structure, but determining that structure experimentally is slow and expensive. Predicting it computationally from the nucleotide sequence is therefore an important problem in computational biology. Our project works on one specific part of this problem: how to find and choose the right templates for predicting an RNA's 3D structure.
+
+A template is a previously solved structure that resembles the target RNA. Our baseline is RNAPro, which predicts structures from the sequence, multiple sequence alignments, an RNA language model and templates. Its accuracy depends strongly on the quality of the templates it receives. This makes template discovery the point where prediction quality is most often won or lost. Different template-search strategies succeed on different RNAs, and the choice of templates at inference is not learned. This is the research gap our work addresses.
+
+\textbf{What our system does.} Our work proposes a framework for improving template discovery in RNA 3D structure prediction. We implement RNAPro's architecture and training recipe and research on finding how templates are chosen at inference.
+
+\textbf{Candidate generation.} Several independent search methods, covering sequence, family, embedding, secondary-structure and motif similarity, each propose candidate templates for the target RNA. The first-place Kaggle method's search is included as a baseline source.
+
+\textbf{Learned selection.} A selector trained on TM-align labels ranks the pooled candidates and passes the top five to the model. When no candidate is useful, a zero-valued template is used as a fallback.
+
+\textbf{Language-model comparison.} We also compare RNA language models inside the same pipeline under controlled conditions, changing only the language model.
+
+\textbf{Evaluation.} We use temporal cut-offs, report template-poor targets separately, and compare results against the paper's baselines and its best-template upper bound.
+
+\textbf{How users reach it.} The framework is delivered as a web application for doctors and researchers. Users submit an RNA sequence, optionally with a dot-bracket structure, and the app validates it and runs the prediction as a background job. They view the result in an interactive 3D viewer with PDB download, see the top-ranked candidates with their scores next to the baseline, and get a low/medium/high confidence indicator. Run history, a methodology page and clear messages for failure cases such as ``no close template found'' complete the core features. Planned extras are a 2D secondary-structure viewer, a ``chat with structure'' assistant, a thermodynamic stability curve and small-molecule compatibility screening.
+
+\section{Problem Statement}
+Predicting the 3D structure of an RNA depends heavily on finding the right template, a previously solved structure that resembles the target, and template discovery is still unreliable. Different search methods succeed on different RNAs, and the choice of templates at inference is not learned. A strong model can therefore fail on a target simply because the right template was never found or never trusted. When this happens, the user receives a poor structure with no warning.
+
+This matters because template-based modeling is becoming central to RNA 3D structure prediction, a core unsolved problem in biology, since an RNA's structure determines what it does. Experimental determination is too slow and costly to keep pace. The best strategy is  a template-discovery pipeline, and ablations of the best integrated model, RNAPro, identified templates as its most important input. The share of CASP targets with usable templates grew from 25\% (CASP15) to 56\% (CASP16), and the authors expect growing structural databases to make templates more important still.
+
+This project addresses that bottleneck directly. We keep the prediction model unchanged and redesign how templates are discovered and chosen. Independent search methods covering sequence, family, embedding, secondary-structure and motif similarity pool their candidates, and a learned selector decides which of them to trust for each RNA. Because the model is held fixed, any gain can be attributed to better template choice. We also compare RNA language models under controlled conditions, and we deliver the results through a web application that reports its confidence honestly, so users know when a prediction can be trusted.
+
+\section{Problem Elaboration}
+\subsection{The Problem in Detail}
+The problem we work on is predicting the 3D structure of an RNA from its sequence, and we attack it through its main bottleneck, template discovery and selection. It has four parts.
+
+\textbf{Automated 3D prediction is not yet reliable.} Computational methods have made accurate predictions in blind challenges such as RNA-Puzzles and CASP, but the successes typically involved human experts guiding the modeling with literature, multiple sequence alignments, prior templates and intuition. Automated approaches, including many deep learning methods, have underperformed human experts. In the Stanford RNA 3D Folding competition, the top automated algorithms reached scores within statistical error of the CASP16 winners, but on the ten RNA-Puzzles targets they still ended just below the human expert team Vfold (0.491, 0.418 and 0.425 against 0.507 mean TM-align). Our system must therefore take a sequence with no human in the loop and return five predicted C1$'$ structures per target that are as accurate as possible. This is the problem we ultimately solve.
+
+\textbf{Finding templates is unreliable.} The accuracy of a prediction follows the best template available to the model. For almost every target in the competition a good template existed in the PDB: 19 of 20 targets had one with TM-align above 0.45. The difficulty is finding it. The paper reports that changing the template-search strategy raised accuracy on some targets and lowered it on others. Its embedding-based search was important for an undisclosed group I intron (PZ67) but missed templates in other cases. For the RdCas12n-sgRNA-DNA complex (9J09), every tested approach did worse than a partially homologous template that was already in the PDB (TM-align 0.511, against 0.426 for the best model). The authors link this to the functional relationship between target and template, which none of the Kaggle models used. No single search method works for all RNAs.
+
+\textbf{Choosing among templates is not learned.} RNAPro takes its inference templates from one fixed search procedure, the first-place Kaggle method. The paper's one multi-model combination picked five of fifteen predictions using a hand-built heuristic score. A heuristic cannot learn from data how reliable each search method is for a given kind of RNA. A failure here is also silent. The model produces a confident-looking structure from a poor template, and nothing tells the user.
+
+\textbf{We cannot yet judge how well this works in hard cases.} Nearly all competition targets had templates, so the paper warns that conclusions favoring template-based modeling may not hold for template-poor RNAs, which remain to be benchmarked. The paper's ten RNA-Puzzles limit statistical power, and its results are retrospective, so a prospective blind test is still needed.
+
+A further question sits alongside these. RNAPro uses one frozen RNA language model, RNet2, and other models exist, such as AIDO.RNA. How much the language model matters, once data, template pool, seeds and training schedule are held fixed, has not been isolated.
+
+\subsection{Sub-problems}
+We divide the work into seven sub-problems.
+
+\begin{enumerate}
+\item \textbf{3D structure prediction.}\\
+\textit{Problem:} turn an RNA sequence into five accurate C1$'$ 3D structures without human guidance.\\
+\textit{Our work:} use RNAPro as the fixed prediction backbone. It takes the sequence, MSA, RNet2 features and templates, and passes them through a 48-block Pairformer and a diffusion module to produce 3D coordinates. Its training recipe stays unchanged, so we reproduce the baseline and report our own number. At inference, the templates chosen by the selector each condition one prediction. We measure the result with TM-align, TM-score, C1$'$ RMSD and per-target Z-scores.\\
+\textit{How this solves it:} the paper shows that templates are RNAPro's most important input and that accuracy tracks the best available template. Prediction quality is therefore limited mainly by the templates the model receives, not by its architecture. Keeping the model fixed and improving only its template input targets that limit directly. Any gain over our reproduced baseline can be attributed to better templates and not to retraining, which makes the improvement measurable and credible.
+
+\item \textbf{Candidate template discovery.}\\
+\textit{Problem:} no single search method finds the right template for every RNA.\\
+\textit{Our work:} run multiple independent search methods and pool their results. These are sequence similarity (including sequence-plus-secondary-structure homology), function or family lookup, language-model embedding similarity, secondary-structure-aware similarity, RNA3DB and motif or local template search. The first-place Kaggle search is added as a baseline source. Each candidate records which method found it and that method's own score and rank.\\
+\textit{How this solves it:} the paper found that different search strategies succeed on different targets, so each method misses templates that another would find. Pooling means a template needs to be found by only one method to enter the candidate set, which raises the chance that the best available template is present. Function lookup and motif search cover partial and functional homology, the kind of case 9J09 shows is missed by whole-sequence search.
+
+\item \textbf{Template selection.}\\
+\textit{Problem:} pooling gives many candidates, and a hand-built rule cannot say which to trust for a given RNA.\\
+\textit{Our work:} train a selector that predicts how good each candidate is, using TM-align against the experimental structure as the label. A candidate counts as useful above a TM-align of 0.45. The selector scores every candidate, removes near-duplicates, keeps the top 5 (tentative) to match RNAPro's five predictions per target, and falls back to a zero-valued template when no candidate clears a threshold chosen on validation data.\\
+\textit{How this solves it:} the selector learns from the training data how reliable each search method is, and for which kinds of RNA. It uses evidence such as which method found a template, how many methods agree on it, and sequence and secondary-structure similarity. This replaces a fixed rule with a data-driven decision for each RNA. The fallback to a zero-valued template stops a poor candidate from being passed to the model as if it were trustworthy. The comparison with the heuristic picker shows how much of the gain comes from learning.
+
+\item \textbf{Language-model comparison.}\\
+\textit{Problem:} the effect of the RNA language model is not isolated.\\
+\textit{Our work:} test other models, such as AIDO.RNA, in the same slot as RNet2, changing only the language model and holding data, template pool, seeds and schedule fixed. Because RNAPro uses pair representations, we first check whether each alternative provides them, and we report any pair features we derive ourselves.\\
+\textit{How this solves it:} a controlled comparison changes one factor at a time, so any difference in accuracy can be attributed to the language model and nothing else. This tells us whether RNet2 is the right choice, and whether the language model matters as much as template choice. It also gives us a clear basis for selecting the best model on validation data, not on test data.
+
+\item \textbf{Evaluation on template-poor RNAs.}\\
+\textit{Problem:} template-poor RNAs are unbenchmarked, and a temporal split alone does not test them.\\
+\textit{Note:} this is an extension that we may add.\\
+\textit{Our work:} use two benchmarks, a temporal cutoff as in the paper and a harder structural-similarity split (planned: RNA3DB). Results for template-poor targets are reported separately.\\
+\textit{How this solves it:} the paper warns that its conclusions favoring template-based modeling may not generalize to RNAs without templates. A structural-similarity split removes close structural relatives from the template database, which creates a test of exactly those cases. Reporting them separately stops strong results on easy targets from hiding weak results on hard ones, so we can state honestly where the framework helps and where it does not.
+
+\item \textbf{Leakage-free and honest evaluation.}\\
+\textit{Problem:} template search over the whole PDB lets future structures leak into predictions\\
+\textit{Our work:} restrict every search, for training, validation and test targets, to structures legal under the split, and score test targets once. We report our own reproduced baseline and the distance to the upper bound, the best prior PDB template. We use confidence intervals or significance tests where the test set allows.\\
+\textit{How this solves it:} if a search could see structures released after a target, the model would appear accurate only because the answer was in the database. Restricting every search to legal structures makes the reported accuracy reflect what the system would achieve on a genuinely new RNA. Comparing against our own reproduced baseline removes the ambiguity between the paper's two figures. Measuring the distance to the upper bound shows how much of the achievable gain the selector captures, and statistical tests show whether differences are real given a small test set.
+
+\item \textbf{Usable and honest delivery.}\\
+\textit{Problem:} a poor prediction reaches the user without warning.\\
+\textit{Our work:} a web application for doctors and researchers that validates input, runs jobs in the background, displays the 3D structure with PDB download, lists the top-k candidates with their scores next to the baseline, and shows a low/medium/high confidence indicator. It handles failure cases explicitly, for example ``low confidence, no close template found.''\\
+\textit{How this solves it:} the selector's scores and the template fallback give the system evidence about how trustworthy a prediction is. The confidence indicator turns that evidence into a simple signal, so a prediction built on a weak or missing template is flagged and not presented as reliable. Showing the baseline score next to the re-ranked candidates makes the effect of our selector visible to the user. Specific error messages mean failures are never silent.
+\end{enumerate}
+
+Together these sub-problems answer the question stated in our problem statement: whether better template discovery and selection improve RNA 3D structure prediction.
+
+\section{Goals and Objectives}
+\subsection{Goals}
+\begin{itemize}
+\item \textbf{Improve RNA 3D structure prediction by getting the templates right.} Research has shown that templates are the most important ingredient in accurate prediction, so we aim to raise accuracy by changing how templates are found and chosen. We keep the prediction model's architecture and training recipe fixed, so any gain can be attributed to better template choice.
+\item \textbf{Make template discovery dependable across different kinds of RNA.} No single search method finds the right template for every RNA. We aim to build a framework in which several complementary methods work together, so the best available template is far more likely to be found.
+\item \textbf{Make template choice learned and data-driven.} We aim to replace fixed or hand-built rules with a selector that learns which candidate templates to trust for each RNA.
+\item \textbf{Understand what drives accuracy.} We aim to show which search methods help, for which kinds of RNA, and how much the choice of RNA language model matters.
+\item \textbf{Evaluate honestly, including the hard cases.} We aim to measure results without data leakage and to report template-poor RNAs separately, so we can say clearly where our framework helps and where it does not.
+\item \textbf{Put it in the hands of users.} We aim to build a web application where doctors and researchers can predict and examine RNA 3D structures and see how far to trust each one.
+\end{itemize}
+
+\subsection{Objectives}
+\textbf{Prediction}
+\begin{itemize}
+\item Train our prediction model following RNAPro's design and training recipe, and reproduce its baseline so we have our own reference number.
+\item Produce five predicted C1$'$ structures per RNA, each guided by one of the five templates our selector chooses.
+\end{itemize}
+
+\textbf{Template discovery}
+\begin{itemize}
+\item Build five complementary template-search methods: sequence similarity (including sequence-plus-secondary-structure homology), function or family lookup, language-model embedding similarity, secondary-structure-aware similarity, and motif or local template search.
+\item Use the first-place Kaggle method's search as a baseline source, so we can measure our framework against it.
+\item Record, for every candidate template, which method found it and that method's own score and rank.
+\item Test each new method on its own, to show which ones help.
+\end{itemize}
+
+\textbf{Template selection}
+\begin{itemize}
+\item Compute a TM-align label for every candidate template in the training and validation data, and treat 0.45 as the threshold for a useful template.
+\item Design and train a selector that predicts how good each candidate is, using features such as which method found it, its score and rank, how many methods agree on it, and its sequence and secondary-structure similarity to the target.
+\item Choose the top five templates per RNA after removing near-duplicates, and fall back to a zero-valued template when no candidate is good enough.
+\item Compare the learned selector against a simple heuristic picker, to measure what learning adds.
+\end{itemize}
+
+\textbf{Language models}
+\begin{itemize}
+\item Run a controlled comparison of RNA language models, including RNet2 and AIDO.RNA, changing only the language model and keeping data, templates, seeds and training schedule the same.
+\item Check whether each alternative model provides the pair-level outputs our pipeline needs, and report any pair features we derive ourselves. Choose the best model on validation data, not test data.
+\end{itemize}
+
+\textbf{Evaluation}
+\begin{itemize}
+\item Evaluate on two benchmarks: a temporal-cutoff benchmark, and a harder structural-similarity benchmark for template-poor RNAs (planned: RNA3DB).
+\item Run every template search, for training, validation and test RNAs, only against structures that were legally available at the time, and score the test set once.
+\item Report TM-align, TM-score, C1$'$ RMSD and per-target Z-scores, with confidence intervals or significance tests where the test set allows.
+\item Compare our results with the published baselines and with the best template that already existed in the PDB, to show how close the selector gets to what is achievable.
+\item Report results for template-poor RNAs separately.
+\end{itemize}
+
+\textbf{Application}
+\begin{itemize}
+\item Build a web application that accepts a sequence (pasted or uploaded, with an optional dot-bracket structure) and checks it with specific error messages.
+\item Run submissions as background jobs with visible status and pipeline stage, and keep a run history with shareable links.
+\item Show the predicted structure in an interactive 3D viewer with PDB download, and list the top-ranked candidates with their scores next to the baseline score.
+\item Show a low/medium/high confidence indicator with a short reason, and handle edge cases explicitly, such as no usable template, a failed or timed-out job, and very long sequences.
+\item Add a methodology page that explains how predictions are produced and where they can be weak.
+\item Add secondary features: a 2D secondary-structure viewer, a ``chat with structure'' assistant, a thermodynamic stability curve, and small-molecule compatibility screening.
+\end{itemize}
+
+\section{Project Scope}
+The scope of this project is to design and build our own system for predicting the 3D structure of an RNA from its sequence, centered on a new way of finding and choosing the templates that guide the prediction. Recent research showed that templates are the most important ingredient in accurate RNA structure prediction, but that no single search method finds the right one for every RNA. Inspired by this, we are building a template-discovery framework of our own. It searches for templates in several complementary ways, pools everything it finds, and uses a selector that we design and train to decide which templates to trust for each RNA. These templates then guide a structure-prediction model that we train ourselves, following the design of RNAPro \cite{leerna}. We also run our own controlled study of how different RNA language models affect the result. All of this is delivered through a web application that we build, where doctors and researchers can enter a sequence, see the predicted structure, and see how far to trust it.
+
+The system will allow users to:
+\begin{itemize}
+\item Submit an RNA sequence (pasted or uploaded), optionally with a dot-bracket secondary structure, and have it validated with specific error messages (invalid characters, length limits, mismatched structure length, unbalanced brackets)
+\item Run each submission as a background job and track its status (queued, running, finished, failed) and current pipeline stage
+\item Obtain five predicted 3D structures per sequence, guided by the templates our selector chooses
+\item View the predicted structure in an interactive 3D viewer and download it as a PDB file
+\item See the top-ranked candidate models with their scores next to the baseline score
+\item See a low/medium/high confidence indicator with a short reason, and clear messages for edge cases such as ``low confidence, no close template found''
+\item Review past jobs in a run history, reopen or re-run them, and share each run by link
+\item Read a methodology page that explains how predictions are produced and where they can be weak
+\item View an interactive 2D secondary-structure diagram, ask questions about a predicted structure through a ``chat with structure'' assistant, see a thermodynamic stability curve across temperatures, and screen small molecules for compatibility with predicted binding pockets
+\end{itemize}
+
+On the research side, we will:
+\begin{itemize}
+\item Build a template-discovery framework with five complementary search methods: sequence similarity, function or family lookup, language-model embedding similarity, secondary-structure-aware similarity, and motif or local template search. Where good tools already exist, such as MMseqs2, Rfam and Infernal, we use them as components inside our own pipeline and combine their outputs with the rest
+\item Design and train a template selector that learns from the TM-align score of each candidate which templates to trust, with a fallback for RNAs that have no useful template
+\item Train our prediction model following RNAPro's design and training recipe, and reproduce its baseline so that every gain we report comes from our template framework
+\item Run a controlled comparison of RNA language models, including RNet2 and AIDO.RNA, changing only the language model
+\item Evaluate on a temporal-cutoff benchmark and on a harder structural-similarity benchmark, report template-poor RNAs separately, and compare against the published baselines and the best-template upper bound
+\end{itemize}
+
+For the application, we will use a molecular viewer (Mol* or NGL) for 3D display, ViennaRNA for secondary-structure and thermodynamic features, and a language model fed with the predicted structure's metadata for the chat assistant. We will fix the front end, back end and job queue during design and record them in the design chapter.
+
+The project deliverables will include:
+\begin{itemize}
+\item The template-discovery and selection framework (candidate generation, selector and fallback)
+\item A trained prediction model and a reproduced baseline
+\item The results of the language-model comparison and the evaluation, including template-poor targets
+\item A functional web-based application with the features listed above
+\item Documentation of the methodology, design, implementation and evaluation, and a user manual for the application
+\end{itemize}
+
+The project scope does not include the following:
+\begin{itemize}
+\item Designing a new prediction architecture. We follow RNAPro's architecture and training recipe, so that gains can be attributed to our template framework
+\item All-atom prediction, since the system predicts one atom (C1$'$) per nucleotide, as in the Kaggle competition \cite{leerna}
+\item Large synthetic RNAs, noncanonical pairs, RNAs with multiple 3D structures, chemically modified RNAs, and nucleic-acid--protein interfaces, which the competition did not evaluate \cite{leerna}
+\item Molecular dynamics simulation, or any validated prediction of small-molecule binding. The drug-compatibility feature is a lightweight scoring dashboard only
+\item A prospective blind test of the predictions. Our evaluation is retrospective
+\item Hosting or server infrastructure beyond what development and demonstration need, and mobile applications
+\end{itemize}
+This project scope will be used as a guide throughout the development of the project.
+
+\section{Sustainable Development Goal (SDG)}
+Our project targets SDG 3: Good Health and Well-being, and it also supports SDG 9: Industry, Innovation and Infrastructure (see Figure \ref{fig:sdg} for the complete SDG list). The structure of an RNA molecule determines how it interacts with proteins, other nucleic acids and small molecules, so knowing it helps biological and pharmaceutical research. Determining RNA structures in the laboratory is slow, expensive and requires specialized expertise, which puts it out of reach for many researchers and slows the work that leads to new treatments. Our system predicts RNA 3D structures directly from sequence and tells users how far to trust each prediction, so researchers can get an early structural picture of an RNA before committing to costly experiments. By improving how the templates behind each prediction are found and chosen, we aim to make these predictions more dependable and so support research toward better health outcomes (SDG 3). The project also contributes to SDG 9 by bringing a new approach to a hard scientific problem and delivering it as an accessible web application that researchers and doctors can use without building their own computational infrastructure.
