+\section{Constraints}
+Several constraints shape what we can build and how far we can claim our results go.
+\begin{itemize}
+\item \textbf{Computing resources.} Training a structure-prediction model of this kind is heavy. The published RNAPro recipe, which we follow, used eight GB300 GPUs, and we need to verify if these resources will be available to us. If full retraining is not feasible on our hardware, we will report the best available alternative and say clearly what we used. Candidate search and selector training must also fit within the compute we can access.
+\item \textbf{Time.} We have less than one year to do the research, build the application and write the report. This limits how many search methods we can tune deeply, how many language models we can compare, and how many of the secondary features (2D viewer, chat assistant, stability curve, drug screening) we can build properly.
+\item \textbf{Data availability and fairness.} Our results depend on the quality of RNA structures, MSAs and datasets. Every template search must use only structures that were legally available for a given RNA, or the results would look better than they really are. This restricts how we build the training, validation and test data, and the test set can be used only once.
+\item \textbf{Limits of template-based prediction.} Our system can only be as good as the best template that exists. If an RNA has no useful template, no selector can create one. We can improve the choice among available templates, but not beyond what the database holds. For RNAs with few or no templates, predictions may be inaccurate.
+\item \textbf{Small and difficult test data.} The number of recent RNAs with known structures that we can use as a fair test is small, which limits how strongly we can claim a difference between methods. Our evaluation is retrospective, and we cannot run a prospective blind test within the project.
+\item \textbf{Language-model compatibility.} Our pipeline uses pair-level features from the language model. Not every RNA language model provides them, so some models may need extra work to include, or may have to be left out of the comparison.
+\item \textbf{Prediction scope.} The system predicts one atom (C1$'$) per nucleotide and does not cover chemically modified RNAs, RNAs with multiple structures, large synthetic RNAs or nucleic-acid--protein interfaces.
+\item \textbf{Usability and trust.} Predictions can be wrong. The application has to show its uncertainty clearly, for example through the confidence indicator and the ``no close template found'' message, so that users are not misled.
+\end{itemize}
+
+\section{Business Opportunity}
+Knowing the 3D structure of an RNA is valuable to biological and pharmaceutical research, but getting it experimentally is slow, expensive and requires specialized expertise. Many researchers and small laboratories cannot afford to determine structures for every RNA they care about. A tool that gives a reliable first structural picture from sequence alone, and says how far to trust it, saves time and helps researchers decide which RNAs are worth taking to the laboratory.
+
+The opportunity grows as experimental structure databases expand. Recent research found that templates are becoming more important to RNA structure prediction, because more solved structures give more chances of finding a good template for a new RNA. A system that is good at finding and choosing templates is well placed to benefit from this growth. The same ideas can also be applied to other prediction tasks that depend on finding similar known structures.
+
+What sets our project apart is where it puts the effort. Instead of only building a bigger or different prediction model, we focus on the step that decides accuracy: finding the right templates and deciding which to trust. We also design the tool around honest communication, with confidence indicators and clear failure messages, so that researchers and doctors know when a prediction is dependable. This is a useful feature in a field where automated predictions have often been unreliable.
+
+Our project is a research-driven final year project, and we are not planning a commercial launch. The opportunity is therefore mainly academic and practical. The framework and the web application could support research laboratories and pharmaceutical teams as a quick structural screening tool, and could serve as a base for future work on RNA structure prediction.
+
+\section{Stakeholders Description/ User Characteristics}
+\subsection{Stakeholders Summary}
+The main users of our system are researchers, such as computational and molecular biologists who study RNA structure and function. They need structural models for the RNAs they work with, and they are comfortable with technical terms such as sequences, dot-bracket notation and PDB files. They submit sequences, examine the predicted structures, compare the candidate models and judge how far to trust each result.
+
+The second main group is doctors and clinical researchers, who work on RNA-related questions such as therapeutic targets. They are less likely to be experts in structure prediction, so they need clear and simple explanations. They submit sequences and rely on the confidence indicator, the methodology page and the chat assistant to understand what a result means.
+
+Pharmaceutical and drug-discovery teams are secondary users. They are interested in how small molecules might interact with an RNA structure, and they use the predicted structures and the drug-compatibility screening as an early indication of which targets deserve laboratory work.
+
+Several stakeholders are involved in the project itself. Our project advisor, Mr. Usman Anwer, guides the research direction and methodology and reviews the work. The project team (Alizeh Adeel, Haleema Butt and Ansa Mubasher) designs and trains the template framework, builds the application, runs the evaluation and writes the report. FAST-NUCES, Lahore is the institution that evaluates the project as a final year project and provides the academic resources for it.
+
+\subsection{Key High-Level Goals and Problems of Stakeholders}
+\textbf{Researchers} want an accurate 3D structure from a sequence quickly, the ability to compare several candidate structures, and a clear sense of how reliable each one is. Today, experimental methods are slow and costly, and automated predictions can be wrong without any warning. It is hard for a researcher to tell whether the right template was used behind a prediction. Our system answers this by finding and choosing templates more carefully, showing the top candidates with their scores, and flagging low-confidence results.
+
+\textbf{Doctors and clinical researchers} want to understand an RNA's structure well enough to support their decisions, without needing expert knowledge of structure prediction. Raw predictions are technical and hard to interpret, they cannot easily judge when a result is trustworthy, and they often lack access to suitable tools. Our application addresses this with input validation, a plain-language methodology page, a low/medium/high confidence indicator with a short reason, and an assistant they can ask questions about the predicted structure.
+
+\textbf{Pharmaceutical and drug-discovery teams} want an early view of an RNA's structure and its possible binding pockets, so they can decide which targets are worth taking to the laboratory. Determining structures experimentally for many candidate RNAs is too slow and expensive, and weak predictions waste effort. The predicted structure, the pocket information and the drug-compatibility score give them a quick first screen, while the confidence indicator warns them when a prediction should not be relied on.
+
+\textbf{Our project advisor} wants to see a well-founded research contribution with an honest, reproducible evaluation. The risk is results that are overstated, leak test data or cannot be repeated. This is why we restrict every template search to structures that were legally available, score the test set only once, report template-poor RNAs separately and compare against our own reproduced baseline.
+
+\textbf{We, the project team,} want to complete a working framework, evaluation and application within the time and compute available. 
+
+\textbf{The university} expects a complete, well-documented project that meets academic standards, with a working demonstration and an evaluation that can be checked. Our documentation, user manual and results chapters are meant to meet this.
