+\section{Conclusion}
+
+This review set out to find how RNA 3D structure is best predicted from sequence and which template-finding methods work best. The literature gives a clear answer to the first question and a clear gap on the second.
+
+Template-based modelling is the strongest route, and template choice is its weak point. The Stanford RNA 3D Folding study \cite{leerna} found that the top two teams used template-based modelling (TBM) without deep learning and outperformed AlphaFold~3 and trRosettaRNA. Their combined model, RNAPro, reached about 0.64 TM-align, and templates were its most important input. The same study shows that no single search strategy worked on every target. Sequence search (MMseqs2 and the first-place method) and embedding-based search (RNAPro-EBA) each succeeded on some targets and failed on others, and for target 9J09 no method matched the best prior template. RNAPro also forms its five predictions from the top five templates with no learned ranking, and its ensemble uses hand-set weights. Other predictors show the same pattern. RhoFold+ \cite{ref:Shen:2024} and DRfold2 \cite{ref:Li:2026} produce pools that often contain a good structure, but the reported gains depend on knowing the true answer, and DRfold2 ranks the correct fold fifth in one case. Candidate selection without ground truth is therefore the recurring open problem.
+
+What this means for our approach. We keep RNAProΓÇÖs architecture and training recipe fixed and change only which templates it receives at inference. Because the training distribution is unchanged, any gain can be attributed to template choice. Our framework has three parts that follow from the gaps above.
+
+Multi-source candidate generation. Since methods succeed on different targets, we pool candidates from independent searches: sequence similarity (Infernal/Rfam and the MMseqs2 baseline), family lookup, language-model embedding similarity, secondary-structure-aware similarity, and motif or local search, with the first-place Kaggle search as a baseline source. Each candidate keeps its source method, native score and rank.
+
+A learned template selector. A ranking or regression model, trained on TM-align labels computed with US-align against the experimental structure, predicts each candidateΓÇÖs usefulness. It keeps the top five templates after optional de-duplication, and falls back to RNAProΓÇÖs zero-valued template when no candidate passes a validation-tuned threshold. This is our main contribution, and its design is a hypothesis to be tested, since RNAPro does not specify a selection procedure.
+
+A controlled language-model comparison. With data, templates, seeds and schedule fixed, we swap the frozen language model in RNAProΓÇÖs gated slot and compare RNet2 with alternatives such as AIDO.RNA and RiNALMo \cite{ref:Penic:2024}. RiNALMo generalises well across families on secondary structure, and BioLLMNet \cite{abir2025biollmnet} shows that frozen embeddings with gated fusion are effective. Neither paper evaluates 3D prediction, so whether these models help here is untested. Papazoglou et al.\ \cite{ref:Papazoglou:2025} found that language-model attention is close to random for tertiary contacts, so we use embeddings as features and not as a standalone predictor.
+
+Where the other reviewed work fits. RiboSeek \cite{wang2026riboseek} needs a 3D query, so it cannot replace first-stage sequence search. It may still help as a cheap TM-score proxy for de-duplicating candidates or as a selector feature, and we treat this as a hypothesis. trRosettaRNA, trRosettaRNA2 and epRNA support producing several diverse candidates rather than one structure, in line with best-of-five scoring. The inverse-folding papers (RiboDiffusion, gRNAde, Ding and Xiao) and Cell2Sentence address different tasks and are outside our scope. Inverse-folding compatibility scores remain possible future selector features.
+
+Evaluation. Because Moyon and Marsico stress that data leakage inflates generalisation estimates, every search, training and validation step uses only structures that are legal under the split. We use a temporal cutoff as the primary benchmark and a structural-similarity split (planned: RNA3DB) as a harder one. We score best-of-five TM-align (with TM-score, RMSD and Z-scores), compare MMseqs2 alone, the first-place search, each single method, naive pooling, and the learned selector against the best-template upper bound, and report template-poor targets separately, since 95% of the competitionΓÇÖs final targets had a usable template. Limits are stated openly: the RNAPro results are retrospective, a selector cannot exceed the best available template, and small test sets limit statistical power.
+
+In summary, the gap this work targets is the unreliable discovery and selection of templates, and our contribution is a multi-source, learned selection layer tested under leakage-free conditions on top of a fixed RNAPro backbone.
+
+\chapter{Software Requirement Specifications}
+This chapter specifies what our system must do and how well it must do it. The system has two parts. A research pipeline finds and selects templates and predicts RNA 3D structures. A web application lets doctors and researchers submit a sequence, follow the job and examine the result. We describe the features, then the functional requirements, then the quality attributes and non-functional requirements, so that a reader can see what was built and why.
+
+\section{List of Features}
+This section lists the features of the system. The first group belongs to the web application that users see. The second group belongs to the prediction pipeline behind it.
+
+\subsection*{Web application features}
+\begin{itemize}
+\item \textbf{Methodology page.} Explains in plain language how a prediction is produced (template search, template selection, language-model features and structure generation) and where predictions can be weak.
+\item \textbf{Sequence input with validation.} Users paste or upload a sequence, optionally with a dot-bracket secondary structure. The system checks valid characters, length limits, matching structure length and balanced brackets before submission, with specific error messages.
+\item \textbf{Job queue with status tracking.} Each submission runs as a background job with a visible state (queued, running, finished, failed) and current pipeline stage. Users can leave and come back to the result.
+\item \textbf{Interactive 3D structure viewer.} Shows the predicted structure in the browser (Mol* or NGL), with rotation, zoom, display styles and PDB download.
+\item \textbf{Top-k candidates with scores.} Lists the best models ranked by our selector, each with its score, next to the baseline score so the effect of the selector is visible.
+\item \textbf{Run history.} Saves past jobs with input, status, date and results. Users can reopen or re-run a job, and each run has its own shareable link.
+\item \textbf{Failure and edge-case handling.} Gives clear messages when no usable template exists, when a job crashes or times out, or when a sequence is very long.
+\item \textbf{Confidence indicator.} A low/medium/high flag with a short reason, so users know when to trust a model.
+\item \textbf{2D secondary-structure viewer.} An interactive dot-bracket and topology diagram of stems, loops and pseudoknots, linked to the 3D structure.
+\item \textbf{Chat with structure.} A language-model assistant that answers questions about a prediction, using extracted metadata (sequence length, GC content, secondary structure, minimum free energy, detected pockets) supplied as context.
+\end{itemize}
+
+\subsection*{Prediction pipeline features}
+\begin{itemize}
+\item \textbf{Multi-method template discovery.} Five complementary search methods (sequence similarity, function or family lookup, language-model embedding similarity, secondary-structure-aware similarity, and motif or local search), plus the first-place Kaggle search as a baseline source. Every candidate records the method that found it and that method's score and rank.
+\item \textbf{Learned template selector.} Scores the pooled candidates, removes near-duplicates, keeps the top five and falls back to a zero-valued template when none is good enough.
+\item \textbf{Structure prediction.} Five predicted C1$'$ structures per sequence, each guided by one selected template.
+\item \textbf{Language-model comparison.} A controlled way to swap the RNA language model while keeping everything else fixed.
+\item \textbf{Evaluation tooling.} TM-align scoring and reporting on temporal and structural-similarity benchmarks, with template-poor targets reported separately.
+\end{itemize}
+
+\section{Functional Requirements}
+This section describes the external behavior of the system. Each requirement states the function, what it does, and who uses it. The users are the User (a researcher, doctor or drug-discovery researcher using the web application), the System (the automated backend), and the Project team (for the research evaluation).
+
+\subsection*{Input and job management}
+\begin{itemize}
+\item \textbf{FR-1: Submit a sequence.} The user pastes or uploads an RNA sequence, optionally with a dot-bracket structure. \textit{Actor: User.}
+\item \textbf{FR-2: Validate input.} The system rejects invalid input before submission and states the exact problem, for example `position 42 contains an invalid character'', with checks for characters, length limit, matching structure length and balanced brackets. \textit{Actors: User, System.}
+\item \textbf{FR-3: Create a background job.} The system accepts a valid submission, creates a job and runs it without blocking the user. \textit{Actor: System.}
+\item \textbf{FR-4: Track job status.} The user sees the job state (queued, running, finished, failed) and current pipeline stage, and can leave and return later. \textit{Actor: User.}
+\item \textbf{FR-5: Keep run history.} The system saves each job with its input, status, date and results. The user can reopen or re-run a job. \textit{Actors: User, System.}
+\item \textbf{FR-6: Share a run.} Each run has its own link the user can share. \textit{Actor: User.}
+\end{itemize}
+
+\subsection*{Prediction pipeline}
+\begin{itemize}
+\item \textbf{FR-7: Generate candidate templates.} For each sequence, the system runs the five search methods and the baseline search, and pools the results with each candidate's source method, score and rank. \textit{Actor: System.}
+\item \textbf{FR-8: Select templates.} The system scores all candidates with the selector, removes near-duplicates, and keeps the top five. \textit{Actor: System.}
+\item \textbf{FR-9: Apply the fallback.} When no candidate passes the score threshold, the system uses a zero-valued template and flags the result as low confidence. \textit{Actor: System.}
+\item \textbf{FR-10: Predict structures.} The system produces five predicted C1$'$ structures, each guided by one selected template. \textit{Actor: System.}
+\item \textbf{FR-11: Swap the language model.} The system lets the research team change the RNA language model in the pipeline while holding data, templates, seeds and training schedule fixed. \textit{Actor: Project team.}
+\end{itemize}
+
+\subsection*{Results and interpretation}
+\begin{itemize}
+\item \textbf{FR-12: Display the 3D structure.} The user views the predicted structure in an interactive viewer with rotate, zoom and display-style options. \textit{Actor: User.}
+\item \textbf{FR-13: Download the model.} The user downloads a selected structure as a PDB file. \textit{Actor: User.}
+\item \textbf{FR-14: List top-k candidates.} The results page lists the best models ranked by the selector, each with its score and the baseline score alongside. Selecting one loads it into the viewer. \textit{Actor: User.}
+\item \textbf{FR-15: Show confidence.} The system shows a low/medium/high indicator with a short reason for each result. \textit{Actors: User, System.}
+\item \textbf{FR-16: Handle failures clearly.} The system shows an understandable message for cases such as no close template found, a crashed or timed-out job, or a very long sequence, and never shows a silent poor result. \textit{Actors: User, System.}
+\item \textbf{FR-17: Show the 2D structure.} The system draws an interactive 2D secondary-structure diagram and highlights regions that correspond to parts of the 3D structure. \textit{Actor: User.}
+\item \textbf{FR-18: Chat with the structure.} The user asks questions about a prediction in natural language. The system answers using the extracted metadata of that prediction. \textit{Actor: User.}
+\item \textbf{FR-19: Explain the method.} The user can open a methodology page describing how predictions are made and their limits. \textit{Actor: User.}
+\end{itemize}
+
+\subsection*{Evaluation}
+\begin{itemize}
+\item \textbf{FR-22: Score predictions.} The system computes TM-align (best of five predictions per target, averaged), TM-score, C1$'$ RMSD and per-target Z-scores against experimental structures. \textit{Actor: Project team.}
+\item \textbf{FR-23: Run benchmarks.} The system evaluates on a temporal-cutoff benchmark and possibly a structural-similarity benchmark, restricts every template search to structures that were legally available, and reports template-poor targets separately. \textit{Actor: Project team.}
+\item \textbf{FR-24: Compare against baselines.} The system compares results with the published baselines and the best-template upper bound. \textit{Actor: Project team.}
+\end{itemize}
+
+\section{Quality Attributes}
+Quality attributes describe how well the system performs its functions. The most important ones for our system follow.
+\begin{itemize}
+\item \textbf{Accuracy and trustworthiness.} Our main quality aim is that predictions are as accurate as the available templates allow, and that users are told when they are not. We measure accuracy with TM-align and related metrics. We treat honest reporting as part of quality: the confidence indicator and failure messages must reflect the real evidence behind a prediction.
+\item \textbf{Reproducibility.} A reader should be able to repeat our results. Every template search must use only structures legal under the data split, test targets are scored once, random seeds and training schedules are fixed, and every choice we make ourselves is documented.
+\item \textbf{Usability.} Researchers and doctors have different levels of expertise. The interface should be understandable without knowledge of structure prediction, with specific error messages, a plain-language methodology page and a clear confidence flag.
+\item \textbf{Reliability.} Jobs should not be lost. A job that crashes or times out must be reported with a clear state, and the user must be able to return to results later.
+\item \textbf{Transparency.} Users should be able to see what a prediction is based on, including which templates were selected and how their scores compare with the baseline.
+\item \textbf{Maintainability and modularity.} Search methods, the selector, the language model and the interface should be separate parts, so that one can be changed without rewriting the others.
+\item \textbf{Performance.} Users should not wait on a frozen page. Long predictions run in the background with visible progress, and interactive features such as the 3D viewer and charts should respond smoothly.
+\end{itemize}
+
+\section{Non-Functional Requirements}
+This section states the measurable and constraint-type requirements that the system must meet. Where a figure is given, it is a design target that we will confirm against our available hardware during implementation.
+\begin{itemize}
+\item \textbf{Performance.}
+  \begin{itemize}
+  \item Pages other than prediction results (input, status, history, methodology) should load within a few seconds under normal use.
+  \item Prediction runs as a background job, because structure generation is compute-heavy. Its duration depends on sequence length and available GPUs, and the user sees the stage the job has reached.
+  \item The system should support a small group of simultaneous users, in the range of 5 to 10 concurrent users, with jobs waiting in a queue when the compute is busy. This is a target for demonstration, not a production capacity.
+  \end{itemize}
+\item \textbf{Scalability.} The job queue should allow more workers to be added so that more jobs can be processed in parallel, if more compute becomes available.
+\item \textbf{Reusability.} The template search methods, selector and evaluation tools should be usable independently of the web application, for example from scripts, so other researchers can reuse them on their own RNAs.
+\item \textbf{Extensibility.} It should be possible to add a new template-search method, a new RNA language model or a new secondary feature without changing the rest of the system. Candidates keep a record of their source method, so a new method fits the same format.
+\item \textbf{Reproducibility and traceability.} Each result should record the input, selected templates, versions of the pipeline components and settings used, so a run can be repeated and explained.
+\item \textbf{Correctness of data handling.} Template searches must be limited to structures legal under the data split. Test data must not be used for training or tuning.
+\item \textbf{Security and privacy.} Submitted sequences may be unpublished research, so they should be visible only to the user who submitted them, except through a share link the user creates. The system should not reveal one user's inputs to another.
+\item \textbf{Usability and accessibility.} The interface should work in current desktop browsers. Error messages should be specific and understandable by non-experts.
+\item \textbf{Compatibility and portability.} The web application should run in the current versions of common browsers without installation. The pipeline should run on a Linux GPU environment, and its components and settings should be documented.
+\item \textbf{Availability.} For the project demonstration, the application should be available during testing and examination periods. We do not plan continuous production hosting.
+\item \textbf{Documentation.} The design, implementation, evaluation and a user manual must be documented so that others can use and reproduce the system.
+\end{itemize}
+
