+\section{Risk Analysis}
+This section lists the risks that could affect the project and how we plan to reduce each one. They are grouped into technical, data and evaluation, schedule and resource, and project risks. The likelihood and impact judgements are our own initial estimates and will be revisited as the work progresses.
+
+\subsection{Technical Risks}
+The most serious technical risk is that the baseline cannot be reproduced. The RNAPro study trained on eight GB300 GPUs and does not state every hyperparameter. We will therefore use the authors' public release and checkpoints where available, report every choice we make ourselves, and, if full retraining is infeasible, use the best available alternative and say so.
+
+A second risk is that the learned selector does not beat simple baselines such as naive pooling or the first-place Kaggle search. We will start with a simple model, add complexity only if validation results justify it, and report a negative result honestly. A related risk is that accuracy is capped by the best template in the pool, and the backbone may suit its own training template distribution better than the one our selector picks. We will report how close the selector gets to the best-template upper bound and state the mismatch as a limitation.
+
+A third risk is that an alternative language model, such as AIDO.RNA, may not provide the pair-level outputs RNAPro expects from RNet2. We will check compatibility before training, derive pair features inside the adapter if needed, and report this as our own design decision.
+
+Search tools may also fail or return nothing for some targets, and very long sequences may exceed the training crop size of 512 or run out of memory. We treat search failure as a normal case: the pool uses whatever the other sources return, falls back to the zero-valued template if it is empty, and logs failures. For long sequences we set a length limit in input validation, show a clear message, and report them separately.
+
+Finally, combining several search tools, a 48-block model and a web application raises the chance of version and dependency conflicts. We will fix and record software versions, keep components behind shared interfaces, and test each one separately.
+
+\subsection{Data and Evaluation Risks}
+The most important evaluation risk is data leakage, where a search or training step sees a structure released after the target's cutoff and inflates results. We enforce the split inside the structure repository, so every read is filtered by the target, and we add a test that no structure released after a cutoff is ever returned.
+
+A second risk is a small test set: the RNAPro study had only ten RNA-Puzzles targets, which limits statistical power. We will report confidence intervals or significance tests where possible and avoid strong claims from small differences. A third risk is that results do not generalise to template-poor RNAs, since about 95\% of the competition's final targets had a usable template. We will add a structural-similarity benchmark (planned: RNA3DB) and report template-poor targets separately.
+
+The RNAPro results are also retrospective, and no prospective blind test is available to us, so we state this as a threat and avoid claims of prospective accuracy. TM-align labels for all candidates are expensive and sensitive to alignment settings, so we compute them once, store them with the software versions and settings, and reuse them. Last, some datasets (the Eigen data, MSAs, RNA3DB) may be unavailable or hard to process in time. We will confirm availability early and, if needed, fall back to accessible data and document the change.
+
+\subsection{Schedule and Resource Risks}
+Limited GPU and storage is likely, because training, caching candidates and running predictions need substantial compute. We will precompute and cache labels and features, use published checkpoints, and prioritise the experiments that answer the main research question.
+
+The scope is also large, covering a research pipeline, a language-model comparison and a web application with many features. We will build in priority order (baseline, candidate generation, selector, then web application) and treat the optional features, such as the chat assistant and 2D viewer, as lower priority.
+
+Prediction jobs are compute-heavy and queues may grow when several users submit at once, so predictions run as background jobs with visible stages, and we target only a small demonstration load. Finally, illness, other coursework or hardware failure may delay work. We will hold regular supervisor meetings, use version control and backups, and leave slack in the FYP-II plan.
+
+\subsection{Project and Business Risks}
+Users may over-trust predictions, for example by treating a low-confidence model as reliable. We mitigate this with a confidence indicator that gives a reason, a methodology page that explains the limits, and a rule that the system never shows a silent poor result.
+
+Submitted sequences may be unpublished research, so a privacy failure would be serious. We make each run visible only to its submitter, except through a share link the user creates. The chat assistant may give incorrect or unsupported answers, so it is limited to the extracted metadata of the prediction and presented as an aid, not an authority.
+
+Lastly, the project depends on external resources such as the RNAPro release, model weights and third-party tools, which may change, disappear or carry licence limits. We will record versions, keep local copies where licences allow, and check licences early.
