+This chapter describes the approach we propose for improving template discovery in RNA 3D structure prediction. Our baseline is RNAPro \cite{leerna}, a model that extends the Protenix AlphaFold~3 reproduction with multiple sequence alignments, an RNA language model, and 3D structural templates. The RNAPro study found that templates are its most important input, that accuracy tracks the best available template, and that different template-search strategies succeed on different targets. We therefore keep the RNAPro architecture and training recipe fixed. Our contribution is a framework that pools candidate templates from several independent search methods and uses a learned selector to choose the most trustworthy ones for each RNA. We also compare several RNA language models inside the same pipeline. The chapter covers the framework, data and splits, candidate generation, the selector, the language-model comparison, model training and inference, and the evaluation protocol.
+
+\section{Framework Overview}
+The framework has two stages that share a data split.
+
+\textbf{Fixed prediction backbone.} RNAPro takes the sequence, the MSA, RNet2 features, and templates. It passes them through a 48-block Pairformer and a diffusion module to produce 3D coordinates. Training uses a pool of 45 templates per target, of which 40 come from MMseqs and 5 from the first-place Kaggle method. When no suitable template exists, a zero-valued template is used.
+
+\textbf{Proposed template-selection layer at inference.} Several search methods each propose candidate templates for a target RNA. A learned selector scores the pooled candidates, and the top five are passed to the trained model, which generates five predictions.
+
+The reasoning for this design is that the training distribution stays unchanged, so any gain at inference can be attributed to better template choice and not to retraining.
+
+\section{Data and Splits}
+\textbf{Data.} We use PDB structures with C1$'$ coordinates, rMSA-generated MSAs (with T converted to U), and the Eigen dataset of up-to-date sequences and MSAs for the second training stage.
+
+\textbf{Splits.} Our primary benchmark uses a temporal cutoff: for each target, only structures released before its cutoff date may serve as templates or training data. We add a second, harder benchmark built from a structural-similarity split of RNAs (we plan to use RNA3DB) to test template-poor cases, which the RNAPro study states remain unbenchmarked.
+
+\textbf{Leakage rule.} Every template search, for training, validation, and test targets, runs only against structures that are legal under the split. Test targets are used for scoring once.
+
+\section{Candidate Template Generation}
+For each target sequence, five search methods run independently and their outputs are pooled. A sixth source, the first-place Kaggle search used by RNAPro at inference, is included as a baseline.
+
+\begin{itemize}
+  \item \textbf{Sequence similarity.} The target is searched against a sequence database, and hits with known 3D structures are kept. Homology based on sequence plus secondary structure uses Rfam and Infernal \cite{kalvari2018,nawrocki2013}. MMseqs2 \cite{steinegger2017} serves as the simple baseline.
+  \item \textbf{Function or family lookup.} If the target's family is known, structures from that family are retrieved from the PDB. This applies only when the function is known, so it is optional for novel RNAs.
+  \item \textbf{Language-model embedding similarity.} The target and database sequences are compared in embedding space. The RNAPro-EBA variant is a precedent, since it used embedding-based analysis \cite{pantolini2024} with RNet2 features.
+  \item \textbf{Secondary-structure-aware similarity.} A secondary structure is predicted or obtained, and the database is searched for similar sequence and secondary-structure combinations. Infernal operates in this way, and BEAGLE is another candidate tool.
+  \item \textbf{Motif or local template search.} Instead of matching the whole RNA, structurally conserved pieces such as hairpins and junctions are matched. The Vfold team found templates for local elements such as hairpin loops when no global template existed.
+  \item \textbf{Baseline source.} The template search of the first-place Kaggle method, which RNAPro uses at inference.
+\end{itemize}
+
+Each candidate keeps a record of which method found it and that method's native score and rank.
+
+\section{Template Selector}
+\textbf{Purpose.} Different methods find different templates, and the selector decides which candidates to trust for a particular RNA.
+
+\textbf{Training label.} For each candidate template in the training and validation splits, we compute its TM-align against the target's experimental structure with US-align, using the C1$'$ atom and sequence-independent alignment. The same computation defines the upper bound, the best prior PDB template, for which 19 of 20 targets had a template above 0.45. We reuse the threshold of 0.45 as the definition of a useful template.
+
+\textbf{Features (proposed).} The finding method, its native score and rank, the number of methods that returned the same template, sequence similarity, secondary-structure similarity, the fraction of the target length covered, and embedding similarity.
+
+\textbf{Model (proposed).} A ranking or regression model predicts each candidate's TM-align. We start with a simple model and report the effect of increasing complexity.
 
-\noindent
-\lipsum[3]
+\textbf{Selection procedure.}
+\begin{enumerate}
+  \item Predict a score for every candidate of a target.
+  \item Remove near-duplicate templates, using TM-align between candidates (a design choice we will test).
+  \item Keep the five highest-scoring templates, matching RNAPro's five predictions per target.
+  \item If no candidate exceeds a score threshold chosen on validation data, use the zero-valued template, as RNAPro does when no suitable template is found.
+\end{enumerate}
+
+\textbf{Protocol.} The selector trains on the training split, its hyperparameters and the threshold are tuned on validation, and the test split is used once.
+
+\section{Language-Model Comparison}
+RNAPro's default language model is RNet2, which stays frozen. Single and pair representations from each layer are combined by weighted averaging and injected into the model through sigmoid gates, and only the gates train. We will test additional models in the same slot, including AIDO.RNA \cite{zou2024} and others chosen later.
+
+\textbf{Controlled comparison.} Only the language model changes. Data, template pool, random seeds, and the training schedule are held fixed.
+
+\textbf{Compatibility check.} RNAPro uses pair representations from RNet2. For each alternative we first check whether it provides pair-level outputs. If not, pair features must be derived, and that derivation will be reported as our own design decision.
+
+\textbf{Selection.} Each model needs its own training of the gating modules, and the best model is chosen on validation, not test.
+
+\section{Model Training and Inference}
+\textbf{Training.} The model is initialized from Protenix base checkpoints, with a newly initialized template encoder. Stage~1 runs 50,000 steps on the Kaggle dataset at a learning rate of 0.0005. Stage~2 adds the Eigen data and runs another 50,000 steps at 0.0001. The diffusion batch size is 8, and the training crop size is 512 with random cropping. During training, four templates are sampled from the 45 available per example. The RNAPro study used eight GB300 GPUs. Hyperparameters that the study does not specify will be taken from the authors' public release, and any choice we make ourselves will be reported. If full retraining proves infeasible, we will report results from the best available alternative and say so.
+
+\textbf{Inference.} The selector's top five templates condition the predictions, with prediction $k$ using the top-$k$ templates, which gives five C1$'$ structures per target.
+
+\section{Evaluation Protocol}
+\textbf{Metric.} We use TM-align from US-align with the C1$'$ atom. For each target we take the best of the five predictions and then average across targets. A score above 0.45 indicates a correct global fold. We also report TM-score, C1$'$ RMSD, and per-target Z-scores.
+
+\
+
+\textbf{Language-model comparison (templates fixed).} RNet2, AIDO.RNA, and other models, plus the no-language-model ablation reported in the RNAPro study.
+
+\textbf{Subset analysis.} Results are reported separately for template-poor targets, since the RNAPro study states that conclusions favoring template-based modeling may not generalize to them.
+
+
+\section{Threats to Validity}
+The RNAPro results are retrospective, and a prospective blind test is needed to confirm them. Our evaluation inherits this limitation. Accuracy also depends strongly on the best available template, so a selector can improve results only up to that ceiling, and we will report how close it gets. Finally, the RNAPro test set had few targets (ten RNA-Puzzles), which limits statistical power, so we will report confidence intervals or significance tests where our test set allows.
 
 \section{Conclusion}
-\noindent
-\lipsum[4]
+This chapter set out a framework that keeps RNAPro's architecture and training recipe intact while changing how templates are chosen at inference. Multiple search methods generate candidates, a learned selector trained on TM-align labels ranks them, and a fallback handles RNAs with no useful template. A controlled comparison of RNA language models runs alongside, using the same data, seeds, and schedule. The evaluation protocol compares each component against the published baselines and the best-template upper bound, reports template-poor targets separately, and states its limits openly. Together these elements let us test whether better template discovery and selection improve RNA 3D structure prediction.
