+\chapter{High-Level and Low-Level Design}
+
+\section{System Overview}
+The system built in this project is a template-selection layer placed in front of an existing RNA 3D structure prediction model. The prediction model is RNAPro \cite{leerna}, which takes an RNA sequence together with its multiple sequence alignment, language-model features, and a small set of 3D structural templates, and produces predicted C1$'$ coordinates through a 48-block Pairformer and a diffusion module. We do not modify this backbone or its training recipe. RNAPro is trained on a fixed pool of 45 templates per target (40 from MMseqs and 5 from the first-place Kaggle method). The design goal is to change only which templates the backbone receives at inference, so that any difference in accuracy can be traced to template choice and not to changes in the model or its training.
+
+At a high level the system has six components.
+
+\begin{enumerate}
+  \item \textbf{Data and split management.} It holds the structures and MSAs and defines two splits: a temporal split, in which each target has a cutoff date and only structures released before it may be used as templates or training data, and potentially also a structural-similarity split (planned: RNA3DB) built to test template-poor targets. Every later component reads only structures that are legal under the split.
+
+  \item \textbf{Candidate generation.} It takes a target sequence and runs independent search methods against the legal structures: sequence similarity (including Infernal/Rfam homology and the MMseqs2 baseline), family lookup, language-model embedding similarity, secondary-structure-aware similarity, and motif or local template search. The search used by the first-place Kaggle method is added as a baseline source. The outputs are pooled into one candidate list, and each candidate carries a record of which method found it together with that method's native score and rank.
+
+  \item \textbf{Label generation.} Used only for training and validation, it scores each candidate with TM-align against the target's experimental structure using US-align. A score above 0.45 marks a useful template.
+
+  \item \textbf{Template selector.} This is the main component proposed in this work. The RNAPro study does not specify a template-selection procedure, so the design below is a starting point to be tested and not a fixed choice. For each candidate, a feature vector is built from the proposed features: the finding method, native score and rank, the number of methods that returned the same template, sequence similarity, secondary-structure similarity, the fraction of the target length covered, and embedding similarity. The usefulness of each feature will be evaluated, and the feature set may change. A ranking or regression model then predicts the TM-align each candidate would achieve against the target's experimental structure. We start with a simple model and report the effect of increasing complexity. Candidates are ranked by predicted score, and the five highest-scoring templates are kept in order, since prediction $k$ uses the top-$k$ templates. Removing near-duplicate templates, using TM-align between candidates, is a design choice that will be tested. If no candidate exceeds a score threshold chosen on validation data, the selector returns the zero-valued template, following RNAPro's handling of targets with no suitable template. Whether this fallback improves results will be evaluated. The selector is trained on the training split, its hyperparameters and threshold are tuned on validation, and the test split is used once.
+
+  \item \textbf{Prediction backbone.} It receives the ordered templates and generates five C1$'$ structures per target. Prediction $k$ is conditioned on the top-$k$ templates. Each selected template is passed through a template encoder, which extracts distogram features from its C1$'$ coordinates, and these features are added to the pair representation. The enriched single and pair representations are refined by a 48-block Pairformer, so that evidence from the template, the MSA and the language model is combined into one consistent description of the target. A diffusion module then takes the refined representations and generates 3D C1$'$ coordinates. The same trained model is run five times with different template inputs, which gives five distinct candidate structures for each target.
+
+  \item \textbf{Evaluation.} It scores the outputs with US-align, takes the best of the five predictions per target, and aggregates results across targets, subsets (with template-poor targets reported separately), and experimental conditions.
+\end{enumerate}
+
+The same organization supports a second experiment, the language-model comparison. Here the template selection stays fixed and only the language-model module of the backbone is swapped, so the design treats the language model as an interchangeable slot with a defined interface and not as a hard-coded part of the network. The design is modular so that every experimental condition in the evaluation protocol, from MMseqs2 alone through to the learned selector, can be produced by changing one component while the rest of the pipeline stays identical.
+
+At the lower level of design, the data flowing between components is kept simple. A target is represented by its sequence, its cutoff date, and its experimental structure when one is available for labeling. A candidate is represented by the identifier of its source structure, the method that found it, its native score and rank, and its feature vector. A selection result is an ordered list of up to five template identifiers, or a flag indicating that the zero-valued template should be used. Every search and every training or scoring step reads from a structure database filtered by the split rule, so the leakage constraint is enforced at the point where data is read and not left to later checking.
+
+\section{Design Considerations}
+
+\subsection{Assumptions and Dependencies}
+The design rests on a small number of explicit assumptions. The first is that the RNAPro architecture, its training recipe, and its public release are available and reproducible. We assume that hyperparameters the RNAPro study does not specify can be taken from the authors' release, and that where this is not possible, our own choices will be recorded and reported.
+
+The design also depends on external software and data. Template search depends on MMseqs2, Infernal with Rfam, and an embedding model for similarity, and the secondary-structure-aware search depends on a tool that can predict or supply a secondary structure for the target. Labels and evaluation depend on US-align, which is used to compute TM-align on the C1$'$ atom with sequence-independent alignment. Training and inference depend on PDB structures, rMSA-generated alignments, and the Eigen dataset, and on the Protenix base checkpoints used for initialization. For the language-model comparison we assume each alternative model can be loaded and run locally, and that it either provides pair-level outputs or allows pair features to be derived, which would then be reported as our own design decision.
+
+On the hardware side, RNAPro was trained on eight GB300 GPUs, and we assume access to far less compute. This is treated as a dependency and not as a hidden detail, because it determines whether full retraining is possible. The users of the system are researchers and the FYP evaluation panel and not members of the public, so we assume they are comfortable with command-line workflows and with reading standard structural metrics such as TM-align and RMSD. We also allow for probable changes in functionality: the list of search methods is expected to grow or shrink after pilot experiments, the choice of selector model will be refined from a simple baseline, and additional language models will be added as they are identified.
+
+\subsection{General Constraints}
+The most significant constraint is compute. Retraining the backbone at the scale used in the RNAPro study is likely beyond the available hardware, and this directly shaped the design. By keeping the backbone and its training distribution fixed and placing all new work at inference, the system needs only a selector that is cheap to train, since it learns from precomputed features and TM-align labels. Where the language-model comparison does require retraining the gating modules, the freezing of the language model keeps that cost limited to the gates. If even this proves infeasible, the design allows reporting results from the best available alternative, with the limitation stated openly.
+
+A second constraint comes from data integrity. Because the evaluation claims to measure the effect of template choice, any template, training example, or selector label that is not legal under the split would invalidate the results. The design therefore requires that every search for training, validation, and test targets run only against structures released before that target's cutoff, and that test targets are used for scoring once. This constraint affects the structure of the code, since the database access layer must apply the split filter itself. A related constraint is label cost. Computing TM-align between every candidate and every target structure for the training and validation splits is expensive, so labels are computed once, stored, and reused across selector experiments.
+
+Further constraints come from the problem itself. The accuracy ceiling set by the best available template means the selector can improve results only up to that bound, and the design reports how close it comes. The RNAPro test set is small, with ten RNA-Puzzles targets, which limits statistical power, so we add a second, structurally split benchmark that includes template-poor RNAs, and report confidence intervals or significance tests where possible. Pooling templates from different tools raises an interoperability problem. Every candidate must be converted to the template format RNAPro expects, with residue mappings to the target, and each tool scores its hits on a different native scale. We therefore store each native score alongside the method that produced it and do not compare scores directly across methods, leaving the selector to learn how far to trust each source. Finally, to attribute any gain to template selection and not to other changes, the backbone stays fixed, and all compared conditions use the same random seeds, recorded software versions and training schedule.
+
+\subsection{Goals and Guidelines}
+The first guiding principle is controlled comparison. Every design decision favors the ability to say that a measured difference came from one cause. This is why the backbone and training recipe stay fixed, why the language-model comparison changes only the language model, and why the evaluation protocol builds up from the MMseqs2 baseline through each new method alone, a simple heuristic picker, and finally the learned selector. A reader who sees an improvement from the learned selector can then compare it against the heuristic picker and judge whether the learning step itself contributed, and not only the pooling of methods.
+
+The second principle is simplicity first. The selector begins as a simple model, and its complexity is increased only if validation results justify it, with the effect of that increase reported. This follows the view that a simple, well-understood model is easier to debug and trust, and that it is better to show what additional complexity buys than to assume it helps. The same principle applies to the deduplication step and the fallback threshold, which are described as design choices that will be tested.
+
+The third principle is modularity, which keeps the pipeline extensible. Each search method sits behind the same interface and returns candidates in the same format, so a method can be added, removed, or evaluated alone without changing the rest of the system. The language model is treated the same way. This matters for the project because the set of search methods and language models is expected to change as experiments proceed. The fourth principle is honest reporting. The design requires the system to report its own reproduced baseline number and not either of the two figures given in the RNAPro study, to report template-poor targets separately, and to state where its own design choices depart from the published work. These goals are included because they affect how results are logged and presented, and not only how the code is written.
+
+\subsection{Development Methods}
+The project follows an experiment-driven, iterative method suited to research work. Development proceeds in stages, each producing a result that decides the next step. The first stage reproduces the baseline with RNAPro's template pool, so that all later comparisons are made against our own reproduced number. The second stage builds the candidate generators one at a time and evaluates each alone, which shows which methods find useful templates and on which kinds of target. The third stage builds the feature set and trains the selector, first with a simple heuristic picker and then with the learned model. The fourth stage runs the language-model comparison under fixed conditions. In every stage, hyperparameters and thresholds are tuned on validation data and the test split is touched once at the end.
+
+We considered two alternatives to this approach. The first was to retrain or modify the RNAPro model itself, for instance by adding the new templates to the training pool. This would let the model adapt to the new template distribution but would require compute we do not have, and it would make it impossible to separate the effect of template choice from the effect of retraining, so it was not adopted. The second was a purely heuristic combination of search methods, such as taking the top hit from each. We keep this as the simple heuristic picker in the evaluation, because it is a necessary comparison point, but we do not rely on it, since the finding that different methods succeed on different targets suggests a fixed rule cannot adapt to each RNA. The learned selector was chosen because it can use features such as agreement between methods and coverage of the target length to decide which candidates to trust case by case. The learning component follows standard supervised practice, with labels from TM-align, a train, validation, and test protocol, and reporting of confidence intervals, and the structural methods and metrics follow the cited work \cite{kalvari2018,nawrocki2013,steinegger2017,leerna}.
+
+\section{System Architecture}
+The system is partitioned into six components. Five run in sequence from a target RNA to a final score, and the sixth is the data layer that all of them read from.
+
+\begin{itemize}
+  \item \textbf{Data and split management.} This is a split-aware structure repository. It holds the PDB structures, MSAs, and stored TM-align labels, and it defines the temporal split and the structural-similarity split. Every read is filtered by the split rule, so a component can only see structures that are legal for the target it is working on.
+  \item \textbf{Candidate generation.} Given a target RNA, this component runs independent search methods against the legal structures and returns one pooled list of candidate templates. Each candidate records the method that found it and that method's native score and rank.
+  \item \textbf{Template selector.} This component has two parts. A feature builder converts each candidate into a numeric description. A ranking or regression model then predicts each candidate's TM-align, and the selector ranks the candidates, removes near-duplicates, and keeps an ordered set of at most five templates. If no candidate passes a threshold tuned on validation data, it returns the zero-valued template.
+  \item \textbf{Label generation.} Used only in training and validation, this component computes TM-align with US-align between each candidate and the target's experimental structure. These values are the selector's training labels.
+  \item \textbf{Prediction backbone.} This is the existing RNAPro model. It receives the sequence, MSA, language-model features, and the ordered templates, and produces five C1$'$ structures, with prediction $k$ using the top-$k$ templates.
+  \item \textbf{Evaluation.} This component scores the predictions against the experimental structures with US-align, takes the best of five per target, and aggregates results across targets, subsets (with template-poor targets reported separately), and experimental conditions.
+\end{itemize}
+
+Only the candidate generation, template selector, label generation, and evaluation components are newly developed. The data layer is new in how it applies the split rule, but it holds existing data. The prediction backbone is a pre-existing part of the system, so it is described only as far as needed to explain how it connects to the new parts. Its interface is narrow: it accepts a sequence, its MSA and language-model features, and up to five templates, and returns coordinates. The new components never reach inside it, and this boundary is what lets any change in accuracy be attributed to template choice. The language-model module is the one exception, because the language-model comparison requires swapping it, so it is exposed as an interchangeable slot with a defined interface.
+
+The pipeline has three data paths that share components.
+
+\begin{itemize}
+  \item \textbf{Backbone training path.} RNAPro is trained following the published recipe, on the fixed pool of 45 templates per target (40 from MMseqs and 5 from the first-place Kaggle method), with the zero-valued template used when no suitable template exists. This path does not involve the selector, and its training distribution is unchanged.
+  \item \textbf{Selector training path.} For targets in the training and validation splits, candidates and their features are paired with TM-align labels from the label generation component. The selector is trained on the training split, and its hyperparameters and threshold are tuned on validation.
+  \item \textbf{Inference path.} A target enters candidate generation and leaves as a list of candidates. The selector attaches features, reduces the list to an ordered set of at most five template identifiers (or a flag requesting the zero-valued template), and the backbone produces five structures. Evaluation compares these with the experimental structure. The test split is used once on this path.
+\end{itemize}
+
+The selector training path and the inference path share the same candidate generation and feature construction, so the selector is trained on the same kind of input it will see at inference.
+
+We chose this decomposition for three reasons. First, it follows the natural stages of the problem, which are finding, describing, choosing, predicting, and scoring, so each component has one responsibility and can be tested separately. Second, it places the boundary between existing and new code at the backbone, which keeps the experiment controlled. Third, it lets every condition in the evaluation protocol, from MMseqs2 alone to the learned selector, be produced by changing one component while the rest stays fixed.
+
+We considered two alternatives. One was a single end-to-end model that learns to retrieve and weight templates inside the network. This was rejected because it would require retraining the backbone, which would blur the effect of template choice with the effect of retraining. The other was to merge search and selection into one component that returns only the best hits. This was rejected because it hides per-method information (native score, rank, and agreement between methods) that the selector uses as features.
+
