+
+\subsection{Subsystem Architecture}
+The candidate generation subsystem merits closer description, because it is made up of several independent parts. It consists of a set of template source modules, one for each search method, and a pooling module. The sources are sequence similarity (covering MMseqs2 and Rfam with Infernal), family lookup, language-model embedding similarity, secondary-structure-aware similarity, motif or local search, and the first-place Kaggle search as a baseline source. Each source takes the same input, a target and the legal structure database, and returns candidates in the same format, each carrying the identifier of the source structure, a residue mapping to the target, the method name, and that method's native score and rank. Sources do not call each other and share no state, which allows them to run independently and in parallel. The pooling module merges the outputs, recognizes when two methods have returned the same template, and records how many methods agreed. This agreement count is one of the selector's features.
+
+The selection subsystem also contains distinct parts. A scoring module applies the trained ranking or regression model to each candidate's feature vector. A deduplication module then computes TM-align between high-scoring candidates and drops those that are near copies of a better-scored one, so the five kept templates are diverse and do not repeat the same fold. A thresholding module compares the top score with the value tuned on validation data and decides whether to use the zero-valued template instead. These three modules run in a fixed order, and each can be switched off for ablation, which lets us report the effect of deduplication and of the fallback separately.
+
+\section{Architectural Strategies}
+
+\subsection{Fixed Backbone, Changes at Inference Only}
+The central strategy is to leave RNAPro's architecture, training recipe, and training template pool unchanged, and to place all new work at inference. This supports the goal of controlled comparison, because the training distribution is identical across all conditions, so a gain can be attributed to template choice. It also fits the compute constraint, since training the selector on precomputed features and labels is far cheaper than retraining a model of this size. The main alternative was to enlarge the training pool with the new templates so the model could adapt to them. That might yield higher accuracy, but it needs resources we do not have and removes the clean attribution, so we did not adopt it. The cost of the chosen strategy is that the backbone may be better suited to the distribution of templates it was trained on than to those our selector picks. We accept this and note it as a limitation.
+
+\subsection{Common Interface for Interchangeable Components}
+The second strategy is to hide each template source and each language model behind a common interface. Every source implements the same operation, returning candidates in the shared format for a given target, and every language-model adapter exposes the same single and pair representations to the backbone. The reasoning is that the set of methods and models is expected to change after pilot experiments, and a new one should be addable without touching the pipeline. We considered writing each method directly into the pipeline, which would have been faster at first, but it would make ablations error-prone and would make it hard to evaluate one method alone. This approach resembles the strategy pattern, in which several algorithms with the same signature can be substituted at run time. For language models without native pair-level outputs, the adapter is where pair features are derived, so that decision stays in one place and is reported as our own design choice.
+
+\subsection{Split Enforcement at the Data Access Layer}
+The third strategy is to enforce the temporal or structural-similarity split inside the structure repository and not in the calling code. Every read takes a target and returns only structures legal for it, so no search, labeling step, or training step can see a forbidden structure by accident. The alternative was to filter results after retrieval, which is simpler to write but risks leakage whenever a new component forgets the filter. A single enforcement point is easier to verify and audit, and since the validity of the whole evaluation depends on it, we judged the small loss of flexibility acceptable. Test targets are handled by the same layer and are released for scoring only once.
+
+\subsection{Precomputed and Cached Labels and Features}
+The fourth strategy addresses cost. TM-align labels for all candidates against all training and validation targets are expensive, and candidate searches are slow. Both are computed once, stored with the software versions and settings that produced them, and reused across all selector experiments. The alternative of recomputing on demand would make each experiment slow and make results harder to reproduce. The trade-off is storage and the need to invalidate stored results when a source or setting changes, which we handle by keying stored results on a record of the producing configuration.
+
+\subsection{Error Detection and Recovery}
+The final strategy concerns failure handling. A search method may fail or return nothing for a given target, and the design treats this as a normal case. The pooling module accepts whatever the other sources return, and the selector includes the finding method as a feature, so a missing source degrades the candidate list without stopping the run. If the pool is empty, or the top score falls below the threshold, the system uses the zero-valued template, which is the same behavior as RNAPro when no suitable template exists. Failures are logged with the target and source identifiers so they can be reported when results are analyzed. The alternative of stopping on any failure was rejected because a long evaluation run should not be lost to one unusual sequence.
+
+\section{Domain Model/Class Diagram}
+The domain model centers on a small number of classes that mirror the data flowing through the pipeline. A \texttt{Target} class represents an RNA to be predicted and holds its sequence, release-date cutoff, and, when available, its experimental structure. A \texttt{Candidate} class represents a possible template and holds the source structure identifier, the residue mapping to the target, the name of the method that found it, that method's native score and rank, and a feature vector once one has been built. A \texttt{SelectionResult} class holds an ordered list of at most five template identifiers, or a flag for the zero-valued template.
+
+Candidate generation is modeled with an abstract \texttt{TemplateSource} class that declares a single search operation taking a \texttt{Target} and a \texttt{StructureRepository}. Concrete subclasses implement it for sequence similarity, family lookup, embedding similarity, secondary-structure-aware similarity, motif search, and the Kaggle baseline. A \texttt{CandidatePool} class aggregates the outputs of the sources and computes the agreement count. The \texttt{StructureRepository} class owns access to structures and alignments, and its read operations all take a \texttt{Target} so the split rule is applied on every call. A \texttt{FeatureBuilder} class creates feature vectors from candidates, and a \texttt{LabelStore} class holds the stored TM-align values. The \texttt{Selector} class has a scoring model, a deduplication step, and a threshold, and it produces a \texttt{SelectionResult} from a \texttt{CandidatePool}.
+
+The prediction side is modeled with a \texttt{PredictionBackbone} class that wraps RNAPro and accepts a \texttt{Target}, a \texttt{SelectionResult}, and a \texttt{LanguageModelAdapter}. \texttt{LanguageModelAdapter} is an abstract class with concrete subclasses for RNet2, AIDO.RNA, and any later models, and each exposes single and pair representations. An \texttt{Evaluator} class compares predicted structures with experimental ones through US-align and aggregates results by condition and by subset, including the template-poor subset. The relationships are as follows. \texttt{CandidatePool} aggregates \texttt{Candidate} objects, \texttt{Selector} uses \texttt{FeatureBuilder} and \texttt{LabelStore} during training, and \texttt{PredictionBackbone} depends on \texttt{LanguageModelAdapter}. \texttt{TemplateSource} and \texttt{StructureRepository} are linked by dependency, since every source reads through the repository.
