+\section{Policies and Tactics}
+For tool choices, we plan to use Python, since the backbone and its public release are built on PyTorch, and to use the established command-line tools for search and scoring, namely MMseqs2, Infernal with Rfam, and US-align. These choices keep us close to the cited work, which makes our baseline reproduction more faithful. The selector will begin with a simple model from a standard machine learning library, and a more complex one will be adopted only if validation results justify it. If any of these choices change during development, the change will be reported.
+
+For engineering trade-offs, we favor reproducibility over speed. Random seeds are fixed, software versions and tool settings are recorded with every stored result, and the training schedule is held constant across all compared conditions. Coding conventions are modest: a consistent style checked automatically, short functions with a single purpose, and descriptive names that match the terms used in this report, so that code can be traced to the design.
+
+For requirements traceability, each experimental condition in the evaluation protocol maps to a named configuration that selects a template source set, a selector mode, and a language model. This mapping lets any reported number be traced to the configuration that produced it. For testing, we plan unit tests for each template source against a few sequences with known answers, a test that the structure repository never returns a structure released after a target's cutoff, a test that the selector falls back to the zero-valued template when the pool is empty, and a check that our reproduced baseline is in a plausible range relative to the published figures before later results are trusted. The leakage test matters most, because the validity of the whole evaluation depends on it.
+
+For maintenance, adding a new template source or language model requires implementing the shared interface and registering a configuration, with no change to the pipeline. The source code is organized into directories matching the components: one for template sources, one for the structure repository, one for features and labels, one for the selector, one for the backbone wrapper and language-model adapters, and one for evaluation, with configurations and stored results kept separately. The deliverables are built by running the stages in order. The first reproduces the baseline, the second generates and caches candidates and labels, the third trains and tunes the selector on validation data, the fourth runs the language-model comparison, and the last scores the test split once.
